 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "WIGTOWN. In 1998 this small town in Galloway was chosen as Scotland's National Book Town, to give a new life to a place that had lost much of its business. It now has a good dozen bookshops, and a book festival every autumn, in a town of under a thousand people. The books have never once asked for a bigger shop.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_wigtown",
   "title": "Wigtown, Scotland's National Book Town"
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "WHITHORN. Tradition says St Ninian came here about the year 397 and built a stone church, Candida Casa, the White House, which makes Whithorn the cradle of Christianity in Scotland. Pilgrims came to his shrine for centuries after, including, it is said, kings. The priory here is a ruin and the story is still standing.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_ninian",
   "title": "Whithorn and St Ninian"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "THE MACHARS. A broad green peninsula between Wigtown Bay and Luce Bay, the south end of Galloway. It is farmland, bog and long shore, and it has one road in and out and a great many bookshops."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "RED KITES. Reintroduced to Galloway from 2001 after being gone from Scotland for over a century. Watch the sky above the farmland for a forked tail that twists to steer. If you see one, say hello."
  }
 ],
